[
    {
        "content": "<p>This is fantastic! Thank you so much for doing this.</p>\n<p>Quick question: How much do you actually depend on Mathlib? Would it be possible to extract that dependency to an \"outer layer\"? I'm asking because Mathlib is BIG and slows down many development and deployment steps. Could it be reduced to just plain Lean or just Batteries? Perhaps after some upstreaming?</p>\n<p>PS: Full disclosure, I am the primary author and maintainer of <a href=\"https://github.com/fgdorais/lean4-parser\">lean4-parser</a> and I am also a maintainer for <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/batteries\">Batteries</a>. That said, my primary goal is always improvement for the Lean community. I always welcome and support innovative ideas like yours.</p>",
        "id": 609705540,
        "sender_full_name": "François G. Dorais",
        "timestamp": 1783876288
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"119741\">@François G. Dorais</span> Thank you for the kind words.</p>\n<p>I depend on a very small fragment of mathlib:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code>List.Vector</code> for the stream type. This dep could probably be solved by adding a generic stream class and then adding an instance for mathlib's <code>List.Vector</code> in a separate library, as you suggested.</li>\n<li><code>Monoid</code> and <code>Lattice</code> for the grade. These I don't know how to solve (they are not included in <code>Batteries</code>). If I define my own monoid class, then it won't be directly compatible with mathlib's monoid, which is a problem. </li>\n</ol>\n<p>It seems to me like I have to choose between pulling mathlib and being compatible with it, or being dependency free but losing mathlib compatibility. To me, being compatible with mathlib seemed more important, but I'd be happy to change that if there is a general preference to stay dependency free. And of course, if there is a way to have the best of both worlds I'd love to implement it.</p>",
        "id": 609729741,
        "sender_full_name": "Jan Mas Rovira",
        "timestamp": 1783898785
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Why are you using <code>List.Vector</code> instead of <code>Vector</code>?</p>",
        "id": 610079894,
        "sender_full_name": "François G. Dorais",
        "timestamp": 1784030293
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Do you really need the bundled <code>Monoid</code> and <code>Lattice</code> classes? Could you get by with unbundled versions supported by <code>Std.Associative</code> etc in <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/blob/8c9756b28d64dab099da31a4c09229a9e6a2ef35/src/Init/Core.lean#L2475-L2556\"><code>Core</code></a>?</p>",
        "id": 610081245,
        "sender_full_name": "François G. Dorais",
        "timestamp": 1784030662
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Discussion thread for <a class=\"stream-topic\" data-stream-id=\"579630\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/579630-Project-announcements/topic/prim-parser.3A.20Total.20Parser.20Combinator.20Library/with/610081245\">#Project announcements &gt; prim-parser: Total Parser Combinator Library</a></p>",
        "id": 610795041,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1784119069
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>One option for avoiding <code>List.Vector</code> would also be to use <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=String.Pos#doc\">docs#String.Pos</a>; that would almost certainly also be more performant.</p>",
        "id": 610795534,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1784119215
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>And I guess the main reason for using <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Monoid#doc\">docs#Monoid</a> is the lawfulness proof, right? So if we were to omit that we could just get away with <code>[Mul G] [One G]</code>.</p>",
        "id": 610795796,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1784119290
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Also, the totality of the parsers makes it possible to prove things about the parsers. Do you have ideas about proving correctness (e.g. some kind of round-tripping) for parsers?</p>",
        "id": 610797224,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1784119656
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>5 messages were moved here from <a class=\"stream-topic\" data-stream-id=\"579630\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/579630-Project-announcements/topic/prim-parser.3A.20Total.20Parser.20Combinator.20Library/with/609697098\">#Project announcements &gt; prim-parser: Total Parser Combinator Library</a> by <span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"687698\">Chris Henson</span>.</p>",
        "id": 610810783,
        "sender_full_name": "Notification Bot",
        "timestamp": 1784122998
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"873547\">@Robin Arnez</span> <br>\nIt is possible to characterize parsers. For instance, this code characterizes the parser for natural numbers: <a href=\"https://github.com/janmasrovira/prim-parser/pull/10/changes#diff-34b89f90ebfe7f14a1ebbcf1ab152beec59ccc182323f1d4098653eed0b49b69R257\">https://github.com/janmasrovira/prim-parser/pull/10/changes#diff-34b89f90ebfe7f14a1ebbcf1ab152beec59ccc182323f1d4098653eed0b49b69R257</a></p>\n<p>The above is fully generated by an llm, so it may not be the best code.</p>",
        "id": 610946118,
        "sender_full_name": "Jan Mas Rovira",
        "timestamp": 1784154689
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I'm a bit slow reading the details here but this seems like really nice work.</p>\n<p>I'm not really knowledgeable in this area but IIUC monadic parser combinators are a big deal in the Haskell world and this version in Lean is far more elegant than Haskell would allow. Nice!</p>",
        "id": 612315078,
        "sender_full_name": "Oliver Nash",
        "timestamp": 1784799761
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Looks nice! <span aria-label=\"rocket\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f680\" role=\"img\" title=\"rocket\">:rocket:</span> No manual though? Examples are great but maybe a bit terse? Most comments and explanation seems to concern the language being parsed but not how to implement the parser.</p>\n<p>The API docs seem to have some repetitions within inherited doc strings, e.g. <a href=\"https://www.dorais.org/lean4-parser/doc/Parser/Stream.html#Parser.Stream\">Parser.Stream</a>. Is this intended?</p>",
        "id": 612362645,
        "sender_full_name": "Adomas Baliuka",
        "timestamp": 1784814551
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>This discussion is about <a href=\"https://github.com/janmasrovira/prim-parser\">https://github.com/janmasrovira/prim-parser</a></p>\n<p>It is not about <a href=\"https://github.com/fgdorais/lean4-parser\">https://github.com/fgdorais/lean4-parser</a> (except for comparison purposes).</p>",
        "id": 612442315,
        "sender_full_name": "François G. Dorais",
        "timestamp": 1784837453
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>This is very cool stuff! Is this the first implementation of graded monads in a dependently typed language?</p>",
        "id": 612463275,
        "sender_full_name": "nrs",
        "timestamp": 1784847774
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"749978\">nrs</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113488-general/topic/Discussion.3A.20prim-parser.3A.20Total.20parser.20Combinator.20Library/near/612463275\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>This is very cool stuff! Is this the first implementation of graded monads in a dependently typed language?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Thanks! It's not the first, there is at least this other project that has them: <a href=\"https://github.com/jbracker/polymonad-proofs/blob/master/src/Haskell/Parameterized/Graded/Monad.agda\">https://github.com/jbracker/polymonad-proofs/blob/master/src/Haskell/Parameterized/Graded/Monad.agda</a></p>",
        "id": 612658517,
        "sender_full_name": "Jan Mas Rovira",
        "timestamp": 1784926738
    },
    {
        "content": "<blockquote>\n<p>And I guess the main reason for using <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Monoid#doc\">docs#Monoid</a> is the lawfulness proof, right? So if we were to omit that we could just get away with <code>[Mul G] [One G]</code>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"873547\">@Robin Arnez</span> Thanks for the suggestion. I've refactored the code so that the non-lawful variants only require Mul and One (<a href=\"https://github.com/janmasrovira/prim-parser/pull/12\">PR</a>).</p>",
        "id": 612659191,
        "sender_full_name": "Jan Mas Rovira",
        "timestamp": 1784926942
    },
    {
        "content": "<blockquote>\n<p>One option for avoiding <code>List.Vector</code> would also be to use <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=String.Pos#doc\">docs#String.Pos</a>; that would almost certainly also be more performant.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"873547\">@Robin Arnez</span>  I've replaced <code>List.Vector</code> with <code>ByteArray</code> (<a href=\"https://github.com/janmasrovira/prim-parser/pull/11/changes\">PR</a>). Performance was unaffected. If I were to use String.Pos as the index then byte-level parsing would not be supported.</p>",
        "id": 612659800,
        "sender_full_name": "Jan Mas Rovira",
        "timestamp": 1784927179
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thank you very much for this very elegant library. This is really beautiful, a masterpiece.</p>\n<p>You gave me inspiration to implement an effect system on top of your graded monad.<br>\nI think we could build a ZIO like library (scala) on top of it.</p>\n<p>I was contemplating how to implement something like it for some time, but building new do notation was stoping me.<br>\nBut your do notation show that this is very much possible and very elegantly.<br>\nThanks.</p>",
        "id": 612673966,
        "sender_full_name": "Alfredo Moreira-Rosa",
        "timestamp": 1784932001
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>One question. given your <code>Text</code> definition, this seems to be compile time only parsing. Is there a dynamic alternative to Text ?</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">structure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Text</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">bytes</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ByteArray</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">pos</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">sound</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">bytes</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">size</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">pos</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">bounds</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">pos</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">bytes</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">size</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Text</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ofString</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">String</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Text</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">toUTF8</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">size</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">bytes</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">toUTF8</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">pos</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">sound</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">bounds</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 612794677,
        "sender_full_name": "Alfredo Moreira-Rosa",
        "timestamp": 1785042978
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"942100\">@Alfredo Moreira-Rosa</span>  It is possible to run parsers dynamically. Note that the <code>run</code> field in the <code>Parser</code> structure accepts a <code>Text n</code> for any <code>n</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">run</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">},</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Text</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Parser</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Outcome</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ε</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">consumes</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>To make it more convenient to use, I've added this function (<a href=\"https://github.com/janmasrovira/prim-parser/pull/20\">PR</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">runParser</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Parser</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ε</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">String</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Except</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ε</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">...</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>And a theorem to reason about the precise return type:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">abbrev</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Except</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Sound</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">errors</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Necessity</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Except</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ε</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">match</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">with</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">error</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">possibly</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">errors</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ok</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">errors</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">possibly</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">runParser_sound</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Parser</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ε</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">String</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Except</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Sound</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">errors</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">runParser</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 613468982,
        "sender_full_name": "Jan Mas Rovira",
        "timestamp": 1785343528
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"1016998\">@Jan Mas Rovira</span> have you looked into using <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Lean.Elab.Do.DoOps#doc\">docs#Lean.Elab.Do.DoOps</a> to implement <code>gdo</code>?</p>",
        "id": 618033578,
        "sender_full_name": "Francisco Giordano",
        "timestamp": 1787354725
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"668081\">@Francisco Giordano</span> I wasn't aware that existed. Thank you so much for pointing me to it. I've just done the refactor (<a href=\"https://github.com/janmasrovira/prim-parser/pull/28\">PR</a>).</p>",
        "id": 618115736,
        "sender_full_name": "Jan Mas Rovira",
        "timestamp": 1787422104
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Glad to hear! Regarding the parentheses this happens when the continuation has a dependent type. At least when the match is in tail position you can use <code>match (dependent := true)</code>. See <a class=\"stream-topic\" data-stream-id=\"270676\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/270676-lean4/topic/dependent.20match.20inside.20do-block/with/608639937\">#lean4 &gt; dependent match inside do-block</a> </p>\n<p>I wonder if this may be particularly an issue for prim-parser?</p>",
        "id": 618117439,
        "sender_full_name": "Francisco Giordano",
        "timestamp": 1787422592
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I've upgraded to 4.33 and tried to use <code>(dependent := true)</code>. It worked on 2/3 matches. So it does help, but still does not behave like a regular match.</p>\n<p>The case where it does not work is:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"c1\">-- does not typecheck</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">token</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Char</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Option</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Parser</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Error</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">conditional</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">gdo</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">let</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">←</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">anyChar</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">match</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">dependent</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">true</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">with</span>\n<span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">some</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ok</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">gc</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">never</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">r</span>\n<span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">none</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">throw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ge</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">possibly</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Error</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">fail</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>But binding <code>f c</code> outside of the match works (I don't know why, but it looks like some quirk of the elaborator).</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"c1\">-- typechecks</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">token</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Char</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Option</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Parser</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Error</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">conditional</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">gdo</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">let</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">←</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">anyChar</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">let</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">fc</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">match</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">dependent</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">true</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">fc</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">with</span>\n<span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">some</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ok</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">gc</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">never</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">r</span>\n<span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">none</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">throw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ge</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">possibly</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Error</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">fail</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
